t is in `App.tsx`, and is visible on small screens. I see the tab bar is positioned `fixed bottom-0` and has a `justify-around` layout. I\'m checking the tabs\' layout and design.\n\n\n**Verifying the header alignment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the header alignment on small screens. The user\'s description of a \"right nav menu\" leads me to focus on the right side of the mobile header. I\'m scrutinizing the `Header.tsx` file and examining the container\'s styling: `flex justify-between items-center w-full`. I am considering how the padding is applied on smaller screens, and how the right side (profile/bell) should align.\n\n\n**Evaluating the content spacing**\n\nI\'m now carefully evaluating the content spacing on extra small screens. I see the header has `px-6` padding, which I had forgotten about, adding 24px of total padding. If the viewport is small (e.g., 320px width), that padding reduces available space significantly. I\'m checking the main wrapper\'s margin/padding to see if it is the culprit. Then, I\'m checking the profile button and the right-side header elements.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying the drawer position**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the drawer\'s positioning on smaller screens, and if it is intended to slide from the left or the right. I am re-examining the code snippets. The drawer *itself* is not explicitly positioned with `left` or `right`, and the container\'s `inset-0` with `fixed` places the drawer at the left, which is standard browser behavior.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the mobile header**\n\nI\'m now pinpointing the mobile header\'s right-side alignment issue. Examining the right side, the bell icon seems properly positioned with its absolute `top-1.5` and `right-1.5` values. I am checking the container styling in `<Header>.tsx`, and am looking at the `gap-4 ml-4` and `shrink-0` classes which are key to proper alignment on mobile. I need to check the effect of `px-6` as well, since that reduces space.\n\n\n\n**Confirming the content overlap**\n\nI\'m now confirming that the main content is overlapping with the sidebar. Looking closely, I can see that the header\'s `lg:pl-72` padding is intended to clear the sidebar on larger screens, but `main` only has `lg:pl-10`. Since the sidebar is fixed, it doesn\'t push the `main` container over, and this causes the main content to overlap the sidebar. I must fix this.\n\n\n**Resolving the content overlap**\n\nI\'m now resolving the content overlap issue by focusing on the `main` container\'s padding. I see the desktop layout\'s misalignment: the main container has too little left padding to clear the sidebar. I\'ve re-examined the code. The sidebar\'s width is 256px, but the left padding for the main content is only 40px. I\'ll need to increase that for larger screens.\n\n\n**Adjusting the container padding**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the padding discrepancy between the header and the main content area. I see that if the `main` container, containing the content frame, is centered (`max-w-7xl mx-auto w-full`), and starts from the screen\'s left edge, the sidebar will cover part of the content. I\'m confirming that the header\'s `lg:pl-72` padding is intended to offset the sidebar, but since the `main` content does not have similar padding, they are not aligned. I will try setting the `lg:pl-64` and removing padding from the header!\n\n\n**Fixing the layout discrepancy**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the layout discrepancy; the header content is shifted more than the main content area on larger screens. The desktop\'s layout is off, with inconsistent offsets. I\'m focusing on the fixed sidebar, and considering how the main container should inherit the same `lg:pl-64` shift to maintain consistent alignment across the content. The header can now use default padding.\n\n\n**Verifying the mobile header**\n\nI\'m now verifying the mobile header\'s alignment. I\'m taking a fresh look at the mobile header\'s `Header.tsx` right side. I see the bell and profile buttons inside a `flex items-center gap-4 ml-4 shrink-0` container. I am considering if these classes properly handle extreme small screen sizes. And I\'m checking button styles, too.\n\n\n**Calculating the element sizes**\n\nI\'m now carefully calculating the combined width of the mobile header elements, including the profile button and the right-side container with its padding and gaps. I\'ve re-examined the code. The profile button and notification buttons\' sizes combined *with* the container\'s margin, padding and gap, seem to fit within even a 320px screen width. There\'s not overlap.\n\n\n**Clarifying the drawer intent**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the user\'s intent: Are they concerned about vertical alignment, which looks correct due to `items-center`? Or, do they mean the mobile navigation *drawer*, whose default top-left positioning is now clear. I think I\'ve found where the problem lies. The layout has no horizontal classes, meaning the content is placed on the top left. Perhaps that is the issue.\n\n\n\n**Confirming the menu position**\n\nI\'m now confirming the menu\'s position on small screens and checking horizontal alignment. On small screen sizes, the right-side actions menu in `Header.tsx`, with classes such as `px-6` and `flex justify-between items-center w-full` should be nicely aligned and positioned. It is likely the mobile drawer is causing confusion. I\'m double-checking the code to be sure the mobile drawer isn\'t interfering with alignment.\n\n\n\n**Defining the User Roles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the second part of the user\'s request, specifically the addition of a login screen with distinct roles: Admin and Sales Agent. I\'m carefully defining each role\'s access. The admin has full access, while the sales agent will only see bookings, no financial information, and can add new bookings following the same flow as the admin. I need to make sure the login screen includes logic to prevent access.\n\n\n\n**Defining user roles and access**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the \"Sales Agent\" role\'s permissions. They will access \"Bookings\" and \"Calendar\" tabs, with a simplified \"Dashboard\" lacking financial stats, to create new bookings following the admin\'s flow, but without financial details. I\'m focusing on \"Sales Agent\" permissions. I am confirming their \"Sales Agent\" role can book as well.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Sales Agent\'s Flow**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the booking flow for the Sales Agent role. The agent needs to use the same modal or drawer as the admin for creating bookings. However, all financial fields within that flow must be hidden or masked. Specifically, the Sales Agent must be able to input customer details, event details, and all associated information just like the admin but without seeing or manipulating any financial values.\n\n\n"
