    thinking: "**Clarifying Amount Visibility**\n\nI\'m making progress: the `CalendarView` doesn\'t display amounts, which is great. I need to clarify if this view also shows enquiries or just bookings. The sales agent should only see bookings without amounts.\n\n\n**Refining Event Filtering**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got it. \"Usko sirf booking dikhengi\" is the key. The calendar view *must* exclude enquiries for sales agents. I\'ve pinpointed the exact lines to modify in `CalendarView.tsx` (lines 47-71). I\'ll pass a `role` prop to `CalendarViewProps` and filter `enquiries` based on the sales agent role. This precise fix directly addresses the user\'s intent.\n\n\n**Defining User Role Access**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user role management within `App.tsx`, and I\'ve created a `userRole` state variable. If `userRole` is `null`, I\'m going to show a full-screen **Login Screen**. I\'ve specified options for both admin and sales agent roles, with prefilled usernames and passwords for easy testing. The Login Screen will be styled with a \"Banquet Pro\" theme, incorporating deep navy blues, elegant inputs, and animated entries. I am excited to design the login screen!\n\n\n**Designing Login Screen Structure**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the Login Screen\'s structure, focusing on its visual appeal and functionality. I\'ve drafted `/src/components/LoginScreen.tsx` to keep `App.tsx` clean. The screen will feature username and password inputs, an error display, and a submit button. I\'m leveraging `lucide-react` for icons, which are Shield, Sparkles, User, Lock, and ArrowRight. I am setting up the initial state for the username, password, and error messages.\n\n\n**Integrating Quick-Select Profiles**\n\nI\'m making it even better! I\'ve decided to add quick-select profile cards to the login screen for an amazing user experience. Users can just click to instantly log in as admin or sales consultant, which is awesome. Also, I\'m revisiting `App.tsx` now to integrate the login state and restrict navigation items for the sales agent.\n\n\n**Restricting User Permissions**\n\nI\'m now implementing granular access control. My key idea: the sales agent\'s access must be extremely limited. I\'m modifying `Sidebar.tsx` to accept a `role` prop, only rendering permissible navigation tabs\342\200\224bookings and calendar for the sales role. `Header.tsx` will display the logged-in role alongside the avatar, and feature a \"Logout\" button. This is a very secure and elegant separation of concerns! If a sales agent logs in, I\'ll set their initial active tab to \'bookings\'. I am ensuring the admin role retains full access.\n\n\n**Adapting Booking Creation**\n\nI\'m now figuring out the \"New Booking\" process for sales agents. The main point is to make the \"New Booking\" process open a simplified drawer that looks exactly like the \"New Enquiry\" flow, but creates a **Booking** with amount set to 0, and status to \"Booked\" or \"Tentative.\" I\'m looking at `NewBookingDrawer.tsx` now to decide if adaptation is possible, or if I need to create a custom simplified modal. I\'ll need to modify the `onAddBooking` function to handle the creation of bookings with these specific parameters for the sales agent role. This simplified view will create a \"Booking\" directly, skipping the \"Enquiry\" stage for this role.\n\n\n**Refining New Booking Drawer**\n\nI\'m making awesome progress on the New Booking Drawer! By passing a `role` prop, I can hide all the financial input cards and the ledger card summary. The drawer title will read \"Submit Booking Request\". The submission will automatically save the booking with all amounts set to zero and the status set to \"Booked\" or \"Tentative\". This is a clean approach, reusing the existing drawer, but making it sales-agent-friendly by hiding financial details. I\'m excited about this design!\n\n\n"
