  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Addressing Sidebar Overlap**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the sidebar\'s behavior. The current issue is its overlap, hiding content and not displaying the intended background. I\'m investigating how the fixed positioning interacts with other elements to address this display problem. The goal is a clean, accessible layout.\n\n\n**Analyzing Layout Overlap**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the overlap issue! The `Sidebar`, fixed with a width of `w-64`, clashes with content lacking the corresponding `pl-64` (or similar) on larger screens. The `Header` has `lg:pl-72`, but the main content area doesn\'t account for the sidebar, creating the cut-off effect. I\'m modifying the `<main>` element in `App.tsx` to fix this!\n\n\n**Refining Margin Adjustments**\n\nI\'m now iterating on the margin adjustments. The previous fix, the added `lg:pl-64`, feels incomplete. The key is to correctly account for the sidebar\'s width on larger screens. I\'m exploring different implementations within the `<main>` element of `App.tsx` now, considering `pl` vs. `ml` to avoid unintended effects. The `Header`\'s placement is influencing the solution, too. I\'m aiming for a responsive layout.\n\n\n**Optimizing Layout Positioning**\n\nI\'ve refined the positioning solution! Adding `lg:ml-64` to the main content container shifts both header and content neatly. I reviewed `Header.tsx`, realizing `lg:pl-72` is redundant with this change. Applying `lg:pl-64` to the content container\'s `div` makes the header\'s padding normal, simplifying alignment. This results in a clean, responsive layout!\n\n\n**Refining Content Padding**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the padding. After revising `Header.tsx`, I\'ve adjusted the horizontal padding to `px-4 sm:px-8` and removed `lg:pl-72`. This simplifies alignment. Also, in `App.tsx`, I\'ve moved to a dedicated language translation context to translate elements between English and Hindi. Next, I\'ll build a comprehensive language translation!\n\n\n**Implementing Language Switcher**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in implementing the language switcher. Storing the selected language (`en` or `hi`) in state and local storage is the plan. A Language Switcher, maybe a dropdown like \"\360\237\207\254\360\237\207\247 EN\" and \"\360\237\207\256\360\237\207\263 HI\", will be in the `Header` or near the logout button for visibility. I\'m building a robust translation map in `/src/translations.ts` to manage all UI text, aiming for a seamless user experience. I\'m making a complete translations file. I\'m listing sidebar items (Dashboard, etc.) and all other views, headers, and UI elements.\n\n\n"
